WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.614
The ablations are what make the diagnosis convincing.

00:00:03.694 --> 00:00:07.795
The oscillations refuse to go away as you vary
dataset size,

00:00:07.875 --> 00:00:13.719
swap a multilayer perceptron for a Transformer,
scale the model, or add regularizers.

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:17.622
Only genuine variance reduction consistently calms them,

00:00:17.702 --> 00:00:22.571
which is exactly what you would predict if gradient
noise is the cause.

00:00:22.651 --> 00:00:28.425
On the fix side, the moving average works best when
you update it every single step,

00:00:28.505 --> 00:00:34.349
use an initial burn-in period, and anneal the
averaging rate with a polynomial decay.

00:00:34.429 --> 00:00:38.391
Skip the burn-in, or update the average only occasionally,

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:40.831
and its stabilizing power degrades.

00:00:40.911 --> 00:00:44.176
Learning rate decay and giant batches also work,

00:00:44.256 --> 00:00:47.800
but they cost far more compute for the same benefit.
